on oh my pleasure  well thank you for  the answer then the next question asks  what about impairment in driving when  using cannabis well this is an excellent  question because I do talk with  employers and they're concerned about  their employees using cannabis on the  job we also are concerned about people  driving there are no simple road tests  for impairment for cannabis and there's  a vast differences in the amount of  cannabis that a person can have in their  system again depending on tolerance that  I spoke about earlier so this is you  know I think the way to look at this is  like any other drug this is the only  really reasonable way to look at it we  don't tell people that are using opioids  or benzodiazepines or sleeping pills or  any other kind of medicine  antipsychotics we don't tell them that  they can't drive the car and if they are  stopped and not performing well driving  they generally are not going to be taken  from their car and have their card towed  away so this is being proposed now as a  way to deal with doings roadside  screening of cannabis users simply to  take their cars away it's really  illogical because there there is there  are studies that are showing that  cannabis use in driving is really  dependent on how comfortable you are  with cannabis and how tolerant you have  become to cannabis so for a new user of  cannabis experimenting with cannabis  don't get behind the wheel it's not the  time to be driving you will be spaced  out and you might forget where you're  going or or you know just not be driving  well in general the drivers that are  using cannabis drive slower they don't  follow behind cars as closely and  generally speaking they're safe drivers  there was an interesting study that came  out of the National Highway Traffic  Safety Association in 2010 it was a  double-blind  study placebo-controlled with 20 heavy  cannabis users and what they were doing  was assessing the impact of alcohol and  THC on these on these people's ability  to critically track meaning staying in  their Lane  a divided attention and in a stop signal  task where you've got to be able to  break at the proper time and in  cognition and the conclusions of this  NHTSA study was that alcohol  significantly impairs those driving  tasks and THC did not affect those tasks  performances so the present study  generally confirms that heavy cannabis  users developed tolerance to the  impairing effects of THC on  neurocognitive cast performance I just  encourage people to be smart about  cannabis use and my patients will come  and say to me oh if I use cannabis in  the morning I'm gonna be sure to not  drive for the next few hours and I think  that's generally safe and wise to do  other people do use cannabis driving and  are rather safe drivers and if you look  throughout the literature you'll find  that to be true as well thanks for that  question  well if there are no more questions I  would like to once again thank dr.  hurricane gather for his presentation I  would also like to thank lab roots for  making today's educational webcast  possible before we go I'd like to let  everyone know that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through June of 2018 you will receive an  email from lab roots letting you know  when that webcast will be available for  replay please share that announcement  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event that's all for now  thanks for joining us and we hope to see  you again here soon at lab roots goodbye
 

